     1→// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     2→// Thumbnail cache — Android 11+ scroll-lag killer.
     3→//
     4→// THE PROBLEM
     5→// Every cell in the home grid that points at a content:// URI from Storage
     6→// Access Framework forces Glide (inside expo-image) to round-trip the URI
     7→// through ContentResolver, then for videos to spin up MediaMetadataRetriever
     8→// to extract a frame. That produces 200-800 ms of CPU per cell on Android
     9→// 11+, which destroys scroll smoothness.
    10→//
    11→// THE FIX
    12→// Generate the thumbnails ONCE in a low-priority background queue and write
    13→// them to internal cache as plain file:// JPGs. The grid then renders pure
    14→// local files at near-zero CPU — the hot scroll path never touches SAF or
    15→// the video decoder again.
    16→//
    17→// SHAPE
    18→// - In-memory map (id -> file:// path) hydrated from AsyncStorage on boot
    19→//   so cold launch already has thumbs ready before the grid mounts.
    20→// - Tiny per-id pub/sub so a newly generated thumb only re-renders the ONE
    21→//   card that needs it — no cascade through the whole grid.
    22→// - Serialized queue with an idle gap between items so background work
    23→//   never competes with the user's scroll.
    24→// - For images we don't write a separate file; we just warm expo-image's
    25→//   own disk cache via Image.prefetch — the next render is instant.
    26→// - Atomic destination: we write to a .tmp file then rename, so a crash
    27→//   mid-write never leaves a half-decoded JPG on disk.
    28→// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
